<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a graph <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>, a subset <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> is a dominating set if every vertex in <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\setminus S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\setminus S</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to some vertex in <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. The dominating set with the least cardinality, <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, is called a <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set which is commonly known as a minimum dominating set. The dominion of a graph <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, is the number of its <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-sets. Some relations between these two seemingly distinct parameters are established. In particular, we present the dominions of paths, some cycles and the join of any two graphs.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dominating set</span> for a graph <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> such that every vertex <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> is either in <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> or has a neighbor <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. The vertex <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in S</annotation></semantics></math> is said to <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cover</span> the vertex <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> if either <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=v</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in E</annotation></semantics></math>. A dominating set <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">minimal dominating set</span> if no proper subset <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}\subset S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}\subset S</annotation></semantics></math> is a dominating set. The <span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">domination number</span> <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(G)</annotation></semantics></math> of a graph <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the minimum cardinality among all dominating sets of <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly, a graph <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can have multiple minimum dominating sets. For simplicity, we refer to those sets as <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-sets</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Dominating sets, in particular <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-sets, have been extensively researched. We recommend to the reader Haynes et al.’s book <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and several other research works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> on variants and applications of dominating sets.
In this literature we found various versions of <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set such as the perfect dominating set, the double dominating set, the strong dominating set, and the restrained dominating set. In general, each such type of <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set has some specified properties. Here, we list five of the most commonly studied <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-sets along with their properties. Later in Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Dominion of Some Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, we illustrate each such <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Perfect <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set</span> requires that no two vertices in a <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set cover the same vertex (outside the <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Connected <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set</span> requires that the graph induced by the <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set be connected.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Total <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set</span> forbids isolated vertices in the subgraph induced by the <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Independent <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set</span> requires all vertices in a <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set to be isolated vertices in the subgraph they induce.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">clique <math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> set</span> requires that the vertices in a <math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set induce a clique.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Because a given graph may have multiple <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-sets, this article addresses a natural but fundamental question:<span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> How many <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-sets does a given graph have?</span> To answer this question, we introduce the notion of <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dominion</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dominion (number)</span> of a graph <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, is the number of its <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-sets. In other words,</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta(G):=|\{S:S\text{ is a dominating set in}\ G\ \text{and}\ |S|=\gamma\}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is a dominating set in</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta(G):=|\{S:S\text{ is a dominating set in}\ G\ \text{and}\ |S|=\gamma\}|</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, given a complete graph <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is obvious that each <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> covers the remaining <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices of <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta(G)=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta(G)=n</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(G)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(G)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, for a star graph <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=K_{1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=K_{1,n}</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, it is easy to see that <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(G)=1=\zeta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(G)=1=\zeta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> since only the central vertex can cover all of the remaining <math id="S1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> vertices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we illustrate the <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-sets of a unicyclic graph <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Dominion of Some Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="196.85" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 157.48 196.85" width="157.48"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,196.85) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(0,39.37)"><clipPath id="pgfcp1"><path d="M 0 -39.37 M 0 -39.37 L 0 157.48 L 157.48 157.48 L 157.48 -39.37 Z M 157.48 157.48"></path></clipPath><g clip-path="url(#pgfcp1)"><g stroke-width="2.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 103.15 39.37 L 78.74 78.74"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-width="2.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 78.74 78.74 L 77.95 111.81"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-width="2.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 103.15 39.37 L 118.11 13.39"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-width="2.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 78.74 78.74 L 58.27 40.16"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-width="2.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 58.27 40.16 L 103.15 39.37"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-width="2.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 58.27 40.16 L 41.73 13.39"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 78.74 78.74 M 82.2 78.74 C 82.2 80.65 80.65 82.2 78.74 82.2 C 76.83 82.2 75.28 80.65 75.28 78.74 C 75.28 76.83 76.83 75.28 78.74 75.28 C 80.65 75.28 82.2 76.83 82.2 78.74 Z M 78.74 78.74"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000"><path style="fill:none" d="M 86.61 82.68"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 83.34 80.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.58em;--ltx-fo-height:0.37em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:8.19pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.54"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="