<div id="S2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Part (i). This follows directly from Claim <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Claim 2.4. ‣ 2 Dominion of Some Graphs ‣ Dominion of Some Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>. 
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Part (iii).
Suppose that <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3k+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3k+2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(G)=k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(G)=k+1</annotation></semantics></math> and every <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> contains exactly one of <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. From Claim <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Claim 2.4. ‣ 2 Dominion of Some Graphs ‣ Dominion of Some Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> we know that exactly one such <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, so our task is to show that exactly <math id="S2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lceil\frac{n}{3}\rceil}=k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lceil\frac{n}{3}\rceil}=k+1</annotation></semantics></math> such <math id="S2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> contain <math id="S2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that <math id="S2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set for <math id="S2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the reasoning in the proof of Claim <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Claim 2.4. ‣ 2 Dominion of Some Graphs ‣ Dominion of Some Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, each <math id="S2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-set <math id="S2.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{r}=\{v_{3r+1},v_{3r+2},v_{3r+3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{r}=\{v_{3r+1},v_{3r+2},v_{3r+3}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0,\ldots,k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0,\ldots,k-1</annotation></semantics></math>, contains exactly one vertex of <math id="S2.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq r&lt;k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq r&lt;k-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S\cap T_{r}=\{v_{3r+1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S\cap T_{r}=\{v_{3r+1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> then, because <math id="S2.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is dominating in <math id="S2.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cap T_{r+1}=\{v_{3r+4}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cap T_{r+1}=\{v_{3r+4}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; consequently, if <math id="S2.p8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cap T_{r}=\{v_{3r+1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cap T_{r}=\{v_{3r+1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S2.p8.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cap T_{s}=\{v_{3s+1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cap T_{s}=\{v_{3s+1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p8.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq s\leq k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq s\leq k-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_indent">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, if <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cap T_{r}=\{v_{3r+2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cap T_{r}=\{v_{3r+2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cap T_{r+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cap T_{r+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is either <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{3r+4}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{3r+4}\}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{3r+5}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{3r+5}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cap T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cap T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is either <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (since <math id="S2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> does not cover <math id="S2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>), it follows that the <math id="S2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-set for <math id="S2.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3k+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3k+2</annotation></semantics></math>, containing <math id="S2.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are the following:</p>
